  "inline_comments": [
    {
      "body": "Won't other kernels also have this issue? Because this is directly imported from `triton_utils`.",
      "created_at": "2026-10-24T03:56:55Z",
      "diff_hunk": "@@ -28,6 +28,9 @@\n from .clip import CLIPEncoder, CLIPVisionEmbeddings\n from .utils import AutoWeightsLoader\n \n+# Triton kernels can only read globals that are constexpr.\n+_LOG2E = tl.constexpr(LOG2E)\n+",
      "html_url": "https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/62858#discussion_r4365905945",
      "id": 4365905945,
      "line": 33,
      "original_line": 33,
      "path": "vllm/model_executor/models/deepencoder.py",
      "updated_at": "2026-10-24T03:56:55Z",
      "user": {
        "login": "Isotr0py"
      }
    },
    {
      "body": "I checked the other kernels on main, and this is the only one that reads `triton_utils.LOG2E` as a global inside `@triton.jit`. `rocm_aiter_mla_merge.py` and `xpu_mla_sparse.py` pass `LOGE2` in as a `tl.constexpr` kernel argument (`LOGE2=LOGE2` at launch), so Triton 3.7 accepts them. `fast_exp` in mamba's `triton_helpers.py` defines its own `tl.constexpr` inside the function. `xpu_mla_sparse.py` also uses `LOG2E` in host code (`sm_scale *= LOG2E`), which is why I left the shared value as a plain float.\r\n\r\nIf you'd rather match the other kernels, I can pass `LOG2E` in as a `tl.constexpr` argument here instead of the module-level `_LOG2E`.\r\n",
      "created_at": "2026-10-24T13:43:32Z",
      "diff_hunk": "@@ -28,6 +28,9 @@\n from .clip import CLIPEncoder, CLIPVisionEmbeddings\n from .utils import AutoWeightsLoader\n \n+# Triton kernels can only read globals that are constexpr.\n+_LOG2E = tl.constexpr(LOG2E)\n+",
      "html_url": "https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/62858#discussion_r4367300724",
